+# Critical
+
+## C-1 — `LendingMarket.upgradeTo` has no access control
+
+**Contract · function:** `src/LendingMarket.sol:84` — `upgradeTo(address)`
+
+```solidity
+function upgradeTo(address newImplementation) external {   // <-- no onlyOwner
+    assembly { sstore(IMPLEMENTATION_SLOT, newImplementation) }
+    emit Upgraded(newImplementation);
+}
+```
+
+`MarketProxy` deliberately has no admin of its own — the header comment says "the
+implementation owns the upgrade entry point." That entry point is unguarded, so
+*every* address is the admin.
+
+**What an attacker gets.** Deploy a contract whose `initialize`-slot layout matches and
+whose functions are `function drain() { USDC.transfer(me, balance); vault.seize(...) }`.
+Call `MarketProxy.upgradeTo(evil)`. You now control the market contract, which is the
+only address `CollateralVault` accepts calls from. One transaction takes the entire USDC
+float **and** every wstETH deposit in the vault, and it is available from the block the
+proxy is deployed. There is no race and no cost beyond gas.
+
+**Fix.** Add `onlyOwner`, and gate it behind the same governance timelock/multisig that
+holds `owner`:
+
+```solidity
+function upgradeTo(address newImplementation) external onlyOwner {
+    if (newImplementation.code.length == 0) revert NotAContract();
+    assembly { sstore(IMPLEMENTATION_SLOT, newImplementation) }
+    emit Upgraded(newImplementation);
+}
+```
+
+Better: drop the hand-rolled proxy for OpenZeppelin `UUPSUpgradeable` +
+`ERC1967Proxy`, which gives you `_authorizeUpgrade`, the rollback check, and a
+`onlyProxy` guard that also fixes M-1. Given the two-week window, at minimum add the
+modifier and the codesize check.
+
