+## C-3 — `healthFactor` divides before it multiplies: real max LTV is 50%, and healthy users are liquidatable
+
+**Contract · function:** `src/LendingMarket.sol:146-151` — `healthFactor(address)`
+
+```solidity
+return (collateralValueUsd(user) / debtUsd) * liquidationThreshold * WAD / BPS;
+```
+
+Both operands are USD values scaled to 1e18. `collateralValueUsd / debtUsd` is an
+**integer division performed first**, so it collapses the entire collateralisation ratio
+to `0, 1, 2, 3, …` before any of the precision-preserving multiplications happen.
+
+**What an unlucky user gets.** With `liquidationThreshold = 8000`:
+
+| Collateral / debt | Truncated ratio | Resulting HF | Intended HF | Outcome |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| 1.24× | 1 | 0.80e18 | 0.99e18 | liquidatable (correct-ish, by accident) |
+| 1.50× | 1 | 0.80e18 | 1.20e18 | **liquidated while 150% collateralised** |
+| 1.99× | 1 | 0.80e18 | 1.59e18 | **liquidated while 199% collateralised** |
+| 2.00× | 2 | 1.60e18 | 1.60e18 | healthy |
+
+Worked example at wstETH = $4,000: deposit 1 wstETH ($4,000), borrow 2,000 USDC →
+ratio exactly 2, HF 1.6e18, allowed. Borrow one more USDC unit → ratio truncates to 1,
+HF 0.8e18, `_borrow` reverts `Unhealthy`. So the advertised 8000 bps threshold is
+actually a hard **50% LTV cap**, and the borrower who maxes it out is one *tick* of
+price movement from liquidation: wstETH $4,000 → $3,999 drops the ratio to 1.9995,
+truncates to 1, HF 0.8e18 → anyone can call `liquidate` and take 105% of the debt in
+collateral. The user loses the 500 bps bonus plus the entire position's flexibility
+while sitting at 200% collateralisation.
+
+This is also the reason C-5's "set threshold huge" attack is bounded — the truncation
+masks it. Do not treat that as mitigation; it is two bugs cancelling.
+
+**Fix.** Do all multiplication before any division, and drop the redundant `debtUsd == 0`
+special-case only after confirming callers handle `type(uint256).max`:
+
+```solidity
+function healthFactor(address user) public view returns (uint256) {
+    uint256 debtUsd = debtValueUsd(user);
+    if (debtUsd == 0) return type(uint256).max;
+    return collateralValueUsd(user) * liquidationThreshold * WAD / (BPS * debtUsd);
+}
+```
+
+Add unit tests pinning HF at 1.25×, 1.50×, 1.99× and 2.00× collateralisation.
+
