+## C-4 — `borrowWithSig` has no nonce, no deadline, and no signer validation
+
+**Contract · function:** `src/LendingMarket.sol:162-168` — `borrowWithSig(...)`
+
+```solidity
+bytes32 structHash = keccak256(abi.encode(BORROW_TYPEHASH, borrower, amount));
+bytes32 digest = keccak256(abi.encodePacked("\x19\x01", domainSeparator, structHash));
+if (ecrecover(digest, v, r, s) != borrower) revert BadSignature();
+_borrow(borrower, amount);
+```
+
+`BORROW_TYPEHASH` is `Borrow(address borrower,uint256 amount)` — no `nonce`, no
+`deadline`. The signed message is therefore permanently valid and infinitely reusable.
+
+**What an attacker gets.** A user signs "borrow 1,000 USDC for me" once and a relayer
+submits it. Anyone who observed that signature — it is public calldata forever — can
+resubmit it every block. Each replay pushes 1,000 more USDC of debt onto the victim
+(the USDC does go *to* the victim, so this isn't direct theft) until `_borrow`'s health
+check finally reverts. The attacker's payoff is the next step: the victim is now pinned
+at the liquidation boundary with maximum leverage they never chose, and the attacker
+calls `liquidate(victim)` to collect the 500 bps bonus on the whole position. Combined
+with C-3's coarse truncation, the victim goes from "healthy" to "fully liquidated" in
+the same bundle. It also lets an attacker drain the market's USDC float into a set of
+victims' wallets against their will and grief them into forced positions.
+
+Secondary problems in the same function:
+
+- **No `signer != address(0)` check.** `ecrecover` returns `address(0)` for malformed
+  signatures. Not directly exploitable here (nobody deposits collateral for
+  `address(0)`), but it is one refactor away from being so — always check.
+- **No signature malleability guard.** `s` in the upper half-order and flipped `v`
+  produce a second valid signature for the same message. Irrelevant while there is no
+  nonce, but it becomes a nonce-bypass the moment you add one, so fix both together.
+- **No EIP-1271 support**, so smart-contract wallets cannot use this path at all.
+
+**Fix.** Add a per-borrower nonce and a deadline to both the typehash and the struct,
+and validate the recovered signer:
+
+```solidity
+bytes32 internal constant BORROW_TYPEHASH =
+    keccak256("Borrow(address borrower,uint256 amount,uint256 nonce,uint256 deadline)");
+mapping(address => uint256) public nonces;   // append to end of storage layout
+
+function borrowWithSig(address borrower, uint256 amount, uint256 deadline, uint8 v, bytes32 r, bytes32 s)
+    external
+{
+    if (block.timestamp > deadline) revert SignatureExpired();
+    bytes32 structHash =
+        keccak256(abi.encode(BORROW_TYPEHASH, borrower, amount, nonces[borrower]++, deadline));
+    bytes32 digest = keccak256(abi.encodePacked("\x19\x01", _domainSeparator(), structHash));
+    address signer = ecrecover(digest, v, r, s);
+    if (signer == address(0) || signer != borrower) revert BadSignature();
+    _borrow(borrower, amount);
+}
+```
+
+Use OpenZeppelin `ECDSA.recover` (it rejects malleable `s` and zero addresses) and
+`EIP712` rather than hand-rolling. Note `nonces` must be appended at the end of the
+storage layout per the README's append-only rule.
+
