+## C-5 — `LendingMarket.setLiquidationThreshold` has no access control
+
+**Contract · function:** `src/LendingMarket.sol:96` — `setLiquidationThreshold(uint256)`
+
+```solidity
+function setLiquidationThreshold(uint256 bps) external {   // <-- no onlyOwner
+```
+
+**What an attacker gets.** Two distinct attacks, both from any address:
+
+1. **`setLiquidationThreshold(0)`** → `healthFactor` returns 0 for every borrower.
+   Every open position becomes liquidatable in the same block; the attacker sweeps them
+   all with `liquidateAll` and pockets 500 bps on the entire book. Simultaneously
+   `CollateralVault.withdraw` calls `requireHealthy`, which now reverts for anyone with
+   any debt — **every borrower's collateral is frozen** with no way to exit.
+2. **`setLiquidationThreshold(type(uint256).max / something)`** → in isolation this
+   should let an attacker borrow the market dry, but C-3's truncation caps the
+   collateral/debt ratio at ≥1 first, so it "only" allows borrowing up to 100% of
+   collateral value with zero margin. Once C-3 is fixed this becomes a clean drain.
+   Fix both.
+
+There is also no sanity bound on the value even for the legitimate owner — a fat-finger
+`setLiquidationThreshold(80)` (bps vs. percent) freezes the market.
+
+**Fix.** `external onlyOwner`, plus a bound and an accrual:
+
+```solidity
+uint256 internal constant MAX_LIQUIDATION_THRESHOLD = 9_500;
+
+function setLiquidationThreshold(uint256 bps) external onlyOwner {
+    if (bps == 0 || bps > MAX_LIQUIDATION_THRESHOLD) revert InvalidThreshold();
+    accrueInterest();
+    liquidationThreshold = bps;
+    emit LiquidationThresholdUpdated(bps);
+}
+```
+
+Lowering the threshold makes existing positions liquidatable instantly, so this should
+be timelocked even for governance.
+
