+# High
+
+## H-1 — ERC-20 return values are never checked (blocks USDT, and can zero a debt for free)
+
+**Contract · function:** `src/LendingMarket.sol:177` (`repay`), `:204` (`_borrow`),
+`:213` (`_liquidate`); `src/CollateralVault.sol:38, 48, 58`
+
+Every token movement in the codebase is a bare call:
+
+```solidity
+debtAsset.transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amount);   // return value ignored
+principalOf[onBehalfOf] -= amount * WAD / borrowIndex;
+```
+
+`IERC20` declares `transfer`/`transferFrom` as returning `bool`, so Solidity ABI-decodes
+the return data — but the boolean is discarded.
+
+**What this costs you.** Two failure modes, and the README puts both on the near-term
+roadmap:
+
+- **USDT (explicitly shortlisted for the next listing round) returns no data at all.**
+  Decoding a `bool` from empty returndata **reverts**. Every deposit, withdrawal and
+  seizure of USDT collateral reverts unconditionally. You cannot list USDT without
+  fixing this — and you will only find out on mainnet.
+- **Tokens that return `false` instead of reverting.** In `repay`, a `transferFrom`
+  that returns `false` is silently ignored and the very next line reduces
+  `principalOf[onBehalfOf]` anyway. **The debt is written down without any payment.**
+  In `_liquidate` the same pattern zeroes `principalOf[user]` and then hands the
+  liquidator 105% of the collateral. USDC and wstETH revert on failure so this is not
+  live at launch, but it is a one-listing-away total loss and it is not something you
+  want gated on a governance vote remembering.
+
+**Fix.** Use OpenZeppelin `SafeERC20` (`safeTransfer` / `safeTransferFrom`) in both
+contracts, everywhere. It handles the no-return-data case and reverts on `false`.
+
