+
+## H-2 — `borrow(0)` spam grows `borrowers` without bound and permanently bricks `liquidateAll`
+
+**Contract · function:** `src/LendingMarket.sol:197-207` — `_borrow`; consumed by
+`liquidateAll` at `:190-195`
+
+```solidity
+if (principalOf[borrower] == 0) borrowers.push(borrower);
+principalOf[borrower] += amount * WAD / borrowIndex;
+```
+
+`borrow(0)` is not rejected. Trace it: `principalOf == 0` → push; `principal += 0` →
+still zero; `healthFactor` returns `type(uint256).max` (no debt) → passes;
+`debtAsset.transfer(borrower, 0)` → USDC allows zero-value transfers → success. The
+caller is appended to `borrowers` and **can do it again immediately**, because their
+principal is still zero.
+
+There is no deduplication and no removal path — `borrowers` is append-only for the life
+of the contract.
+
+**What an attacker gets.** On Arbitrum a storage-array push costs on the order of a cent.
+For a few hundred dollars an attacker appends millions of entries. `liquidateAll` loops
+over the whole array calling `healthFactor` — which itself loops over `collateralTokens`
+doing an external `oracle.getPrice` and an external `decimals()` call per token — so it
+exceeds the block gas limit and **can never be called again**. The README describes
+`liquidateAll` as the keeper's response to a large price move; that response is now
+permanently unavailable, exactly when you need it. Nothing short of an upgrade recovers
+it, and the array is still there afterwards.
+
+The same array also accumulates every fully-repaid borrower forever, so it degrades on
+its own without any attacker.
+
+**Fix.** Three changes:
+
+```solidity
+mapping(address => bool) public isBorrower;   // append to end of storage layout
+
+function _borrow(address borrower, uint256 amount) internal {
+    if (amount == 0) revert ZeroAmount();
+    accrueInterest();
+    if (!isBorrower[borrower]) { isBorrower[borrower] = true; borrowers.push(borrower); }
+    ...
+}
+```
+
+and replace `liquidateAll()` with a bounded, caller-supplied batch —
+`liquidateBatch(address[] calldata users)` — so the keeper controls gas and a single
+bad entry cannot revert the sweep (see M-4). Enumerating positions on-chain is the wrong
+tool here; keepers should read the borrower set from events off-chain.
+
