+## H-3 — `CollateralVault.withdraw` transfers before updating the balance
+
+**Contract · function:** `src/CollateralVault.sol:44-53` — `withdraw(address,uint256)`
+
+```solidity
+uint256 balance = balanceOf[msg.sender][token];
+if (balance < amount) revert InsufficientBalance();
+
+IERC20(token).transfer(msg.sender, amount);          // <-- interaction
+balanceOf[msg.sender][token] = balance - amount;     // <-- effect, after
+ILendingMarket(market).requireHealthy(msg.sender);
+```
+
+Textbook checks-effects-interactions violation, with no reentrancy guard anywhere in
+the codebase. `deposit` and `seize` in the same file get the ordering right, so this is
+an isolated slip.
+
+**What an attacker gets.** With any collateral token that yields control during
+`transfer` — ERC-777 `tokensReceived`, an ERC-1363 / callback token, or a token with a
+transfer hook — the attacker re-enters `withdraw` before line 49 runs. `balanceOf` is
+still the full pre-withdrawal amount on every re-entry, and `requireHealthy` is never
+reached until the stack unwinds. Loop until the vault's balance of that token is gone,
+including **other users' deposits of the same token**. The health check at the end is
+irrelevant: the funds are already out.
+
+wstETH has no transfer hook, so this is not exploitable at launch. It becomes
+exploitable the moment the DAO acts on the README's stated plan for "yield-bearing and
+rebasing collateral," and it will be a listing decision rather than a code review that
+triggers it. That is not a risk to carry into production.
+
+**Fix.** Reorder to effects-then-interactions, run the health check before the transfer,
+and add a `nonReentrant` guard as defence in depth:
+
+```solidity
+function withdraw(address token, uint256 amount) external nonReentrant {
+    uint256 balance = balanceOf[msg.sender][token];
+    if (balance < amount) revert InsufficientBalance();
+
+    balanceOf[msg.sender][token] = balance - amount;
+    ILendingMarket(market).requireHealthy(msg.sender);   // check against post-state
+    IERC20(token).safeTransfer(msg.sender, amount);
+
+    emit Withdrawn(msg.sender, token, amount);
+}
+```
+
+Note this also fixes a second bug: `requireHealthy` currently runs *after* the tokens
+have left, so even without reentrancy the ordering is only accidentally correct.
+
