+## H-4 — No Arbitrum sequencer-uptime check
+
+**Contract · function:** `src/PriceOracle.sol:39` — `getPrice(address)`
+
+The README fixes the deployment target as Arbitrum One. When the Arbitrum sequencer goes
+down and restarts, Chainlink feeds resume with prices that jumped during the outage,
+and users had no ability to transact in the interim to defend their positions.
+
+**What an unlucky user gets.** ETH drops 15% while the sequencer is offline. On restart,
+the first block contains a queue of transactions including keeper liquidations. Every
+borrower who would have topped up or repaid — and could not, because the chain was not
+accepting their transactions — is liquidated at the new price and pays the 500 bps
+bonus. This has happened on Arbitrum before and is the reason Chainlink publishes an L2
+Sequencer Uptime Feed.
+
+**Fix.** Consult the uptime feed and reject prices during downtime and for a grace
+period after recovery:
+
+```solidity
+IAggregatorV3 public constant SEQUENCER_UPTIME =
+    IAggregatorV3(0xFdB631F5EE196F0ed6FAa767959853A9F217697D);  // Arbitrum One
+uint256 public constant GRACE_PERIOD = 1 hours;
+
+function _requireSequencerUp() internal view {
+    (, int256 up, uint256 startedAt, , ) = SEQUENCER_UPTIME.latestRoundData();
+    if (up != 0) revert SequencerDown();
+    if (block.timestamp - startedAt <= GRACE_PERIOD) revert GracePeriodNotOver();
+}
+```
+
+Call it at the top of `getPrice`. Verify the feed address against Chainlink's Arbitrum
+docs at deployment time. Pair this with a pause mechanism (M-8) so governance can
+extend the grace period manually after a long outage.
+
+---
+
+## H-5 — wstETH is configured against the stETH/USD feed
+
+**Contract · function:** deployment configuration — README "Feed set for wstETH",
+consumed by `PriceOracle.getPrice` → `LendingMarket.collateralValueUsd`
+
+| README row | Value |
+| --- | --- |
+| First listed collateral | wstETH `0x5979D7b546E38E414F7E9822514be443A4800529` |
+| Feed set for wstETH | **stETH / USD** `0x07C5b924399cc23c24a95c8743DE4006a32b7f2a` |
+
+wstETH is the *wrapped* form of stETH: 1 wstETH is worth `stEth.getPooledEthByShares(1e18)`
+stETH, currently ≈ 1.21 and monotonically increasing as staking rewards accrue. Pricing
+wstETH with the stETH/USD feed therefore undervalues the collateral by the full
+accumulated staking yield — roughly **21% today, and growing every day the market is
+live**.
+
+**What an unlucky user gets.** Every borrower's `collateralValueUsd` is understated by
+~21%, so effective borrowing power is ~21% lower than advertised, and — worse —
+positions are liquidated ~21% earlier than the 8000 bps threshold implies. Stacked on
+top of C-3's truncation, a user who believes they have a comfortable buffer is in fact
+one small candle from losing 500 bps of their position to a liquidator. The error is
+one-directional and always against the user, and it widens over time.
+
+This is a configuration finding rather than a code defect, but the code offers no way to
+express the correction: `PriceOracle` is strictly one flat feed per token with no
+composition.
+
+**Fix.** Either use a wstETH/USD feed directly if one is available on Arbitrum One at
+deployment time, or add a composed feed type to `PriceOracle` that multiplies the
+stETH/USD answer by the on-chain wstETH exchange rate:
+
+```solidity
+// price(wstETH) = stETH/USD * IWstETH(wstETH).stEthPerToken() / 1e18
+```
+
+Whichever you choose, add a deployment check that asserts each listed collateral's
+quoted price is within a few percent of an independent reference before the market
+opens. Also re-verify the USDC/USD feed address in the same pass — a wrong feed on the
+debt side is symmetric and just as damaging.
+
