+
+# Medium
+
+## M-1 — Implementation contract is left uninitialised; proxy initialisation is front-runnable
+
+**Contract · function:** `src/LendingMarket.sol:56` — `initialize(...)`;
+`src/MarketProxy.sol:10-24` — constructor
+
+`initialize` is `external` with only an `initialized` flag guarding it. `MarketProxy`'s
+constructor delegatecalls `initData`, which initialises the *proxy's* storage — the
+implementation's own storage stays untouched, so anyone can call `initialize` directly
+on the implementation address and become its `owner`.
+
+**Impact.** On its own this is limited: the implementation holds no funds, and
+`selfdestruct` has no effect under Solidity 0.8.28 / EIP-6780 for an already-deployed
+contract. But it is exactly the state that C-1 turns into a takeover, and if the
+deployment script ever passes empty `initData` (or splits deploy and init into two
+transactions), an observer front-runs `initialize` on the *proxy* and becomes owner of
+the live market before the treasury funds it.
+
+**Fix.** Add a constructor to `LendingMarket` that burns the initialiser on the
+implementation, and never deploy the proxy without `initData`:
+
+```solidity
+constructor() { initialized = true; }
+```
+
+Use OpenZeppelin `Initializable` (`_disableInitializers()` in the constructor) if you
+move to the standard proxy stack per C-1.
+
+---
+
+## M-2 — No close factor: a position 1 wei underwater is liquidated in full
+
+**Contract · function:** `src/LendingMarket.sol:209-234` — `_liquidate(address)`
+
+```solidity
+uint256 debt = debtOf(user);
+debtAsset.transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), debt);   // all of it
+principalOf[user] = 0;
+```
+
+Every liquidation closes 100% of the debt and seizes `debt × 1.05` in collateral.
+
+**What an unlucky user gets.** Aave and Compound cap a single liquidation at 50% of debt
+precisely so that a marginal dip does not wipe out the whole position. Here, a borrower
+whose health factor touches `1e18 - 1` loses their entire position and pays the full
+500 bps bonus, when repaying a fraction would have restored health. Compounded with C-3
+— where positions cross from HF 1.6 straight to HF 0.8 on a fractional price move — the
+practical experience is that users get fully liquidated with no warning band at all.
+
+**Fix.** Introduce a close factor and let the liquidator specify the repay amount:
+
+```solidity
+uint256 public closeFactor;   // e.g. 5_000 bps; append to storage layout
+
+function liquidate(address user, uint256 repayAmount) external {
+    accrueInterest();
+    if (healthFactor(user) >= WAD) revert Healthy();
+    uint256 maxRepay = debtOf(user) * closeFactor / BPS;
+    if (repayAmount > maxRepay) repayAmount = maxRepay;
+    _liquidate(user, repayAmount);
+}
+```
+
+Allow 100% close only below some deep-underwater threshold (e.g. HF < 0.95e18), which
+is the standard Aave v3 behaviour.
+
+---
+
+## M-3 — No bad-debt handling: the liquidator overpays when collateral is short
+
+**Contract · function:** `src/LendingMarket.sol:209-231` — `_liquidate(address)`
+
+The liquidator pays `debt` unconditionally up front, then the seizure loop takes
+`min(collateralValue, seizeUsd)` per token and simply exits when collateral runs out —
+leftover `seizeUsd` is discarded silently.
+
+**Impact.** If a position's collateral is worth less than `debt × 1.05` (a gap-down, or
+any of C-3/C-6/H-5 mispricing it), the liquidator pays full debt and receives less than
+they paid. No rational liquidator will do this, so genuinely insolvent positions sit
+unliquidated and become permanent bad debt against the treasury's float. Meanwhile
+`liquidateAll` — the keeper path — does *not* check profitability, so **your own keeper
+will systematically burn treasury USDC** liquidating insolvent positions at a loss.
+
+**Fix.** Compute the seizable collateral first, cap the repayment to what the collateral
+can actually cover, and only transfer that much in:
+
+```solidity
+uint256 collUsd = collateralValueUsd(user);
+uint256 maxRepayUsd = collUsd * BPS / (BPS + liquidationBonus);
+if (debtUsd > maxRepayUsd) { /* clamp repay, record shortfall as protocol bad debt */ }
+```
+
+Add an explicit `badDebt` accumulator and a socialisation or treasury-writedown path so
+insolvent positions can be cleared rather than lingering.
+
+---
+
+## M-4 — `liquidateAll` is an unbounded, all-or-nothing loop
+
+**Contract · function:** `src/LendingMarket.sol:190-195` — `liquidateAll()`
+
+Beyond the H-2 DoS, the design is wrong even with an honest `borrowers` array: the loop
+has no bound and no per-entry error handling, so a single `_liquidate` that reverts
+(insufficient liquidator allowance, a paused collateral token, a reverting oracle for
+one listed asset) reverts the **entire** sweep. The keeper must also have approved
+enough USDC to cover the sum of every underwater debt simultaneously.
+
+**Fix.** Replace with `liquidateBatch(address[] calldata users)` and skip failures
+individually via `try`/`catch` or a pre-check, so one bad position cannot block the
+rest. Source the candidate list off-chain from `Borrowed`/`Repaid` events.
+
+---
+
+## M-5 — One global liquidation threshold; no per-asset risk parameters, caps, or delisting
+
+**Contract · function:** `src/LendingMarket.sol:107-114` — `listCollateral(address)`;
+`liquidationThreshold` at `:22`
+
+`listCollateral` sets a boolean and pushes to an array. There is no per-asset LTV, no
+per-asset liquidation threshold, no supply cap, no borrow cap, and no way to *un*list.
+
+**Impact.** The README's next listing round is weETH, ezETH and USDT. ezETH in
+particular has materially different liquidity and depeg characteristics from wstETH —
+it has already depegged once in production — and giving it the same 8000 bps threshold
+as wstETH is not a defensible risk position. With no supply cap, a single asset can
+become the entire collateral base. With no delisting, a compromised or deprecated token
+stays listed forever (and stays in the `collateralTokens` loop that `healthFactor` walks
+on every call). `isCollateral` is also written but never actually read by any check —
+`CollateralVault.supported` is the real gate, so the two can drift.
+
+**Fix.** Move to a per-asset config struct set at listing time:
+
+```solidity
+struct CollateralConfig { uint256 ltv; uint256 liquidationThreshold; uint256 bonus;
+                          uint256 supplyCap; bool enabled; }
+mapping(address => CollateralConfig) public collateralConfig;
+```
+
+Compute the health factor as a collateral-weighted average of per-asset thresholds, add
+a `setCollateralEnabled(token, false)` that blocks new deposits and new borrows while
+still allowing withdrawal and liquidation, and enforce `supplyCap` in
+`CollateralVault.deposit`.
+
