+
+## M-7 — Interest is derived from `block.number × 12` rather than `block.timestamp`
+
+**Contract · function:** `src/LendingMarket.sol:117-126` — `accrueInterest()`
+
+```solidity
+uint256 constant SECONDS_PER_BLOCK = 12;
+uint256 secondsElapsed = (block.number - lastAccrualBlock) * SECONDS_PER_BLOCK;
+```
+
+On Arbitrum Nitro `block.number` returns an *estimate of the L1 block number*, so 12
+seconds per block is approximately right today — this is not the 48× error you get from
+assuming L2 block time, but it is right by coincidence rather than by construction. The
+estimate is explicitly documented as approximate and can lag or jump; L1 block time is
+an Ethereum consensus parameter baked into an L2 contract; and the same code deployed
+to Base or Optimism (2s blocks) would over-charge interest by 6×.
+
+`block.timestamp` on Arbitrum is accurate, monotonic, and is the correct source.
+
+**Fix.**
+
+```solidity
+uint256 public lastAccrualTime;   // append to storage layout
+
+function accrueInterest() public {
+    uint256 elapsed = block.timestamp - lastAccrualTime;
+    if (elapsed == 0) return;
+    borrowIndex += borrowIndex * borrowRate * elapsed / (BPS * 365 days);
+    lastAccrualTime = block.timestamp;
+}
+```
+
+Per the README's append-only rule, add `lastAccrualTime` at the end and leave the now-dead
+`lastAccrualBlock` slot in place.
+
